# Append this block to /etc/caddy/Caddyfile. It is ADDITIVE and isolated:
# it does NOT touch the existing `213.176.74.63.nip.io:8443 -> localhost:8080`
# relay block, nor nginx:443 (the SNI stream router for the relay).
#
# A bare `:8088` site serves plain HTTP (no auto-TLS, no cert) on a dedicated
# port, so the router's curl needs no certificate handling. Only the /stats
# path is proxied to the localhost collector; everything else 404s.

# NO TIMEOUT DIRECTIVES HERE, DELIBERATELY. Caddy v2 has no default HTTP
# read/write timeout, and the collector's own socket timeout is not a substitute
# because it resets on every byte received — so a slow client is bounded by
# neither. The fix would be `servers { timeouts { ... } }`, but that is a GLOBAL
# option: it must live in the single global block at the very top of the
# Caddyfile. This file is APPENDED to a Caddyfile that already serves the
# Telegram relay, and appending a second global block breaks the whole config —
# the relay included. Editing the existing global block in place is not worth the
# risk to a service people depend on.
#
# What bounds slow clients instead is MAX_CONN in the collector: a ceiling on
# concurrent connections makes the attack finite regardless of how long any one
# of them lingers. If timeouts are ever wanted here, add them to the global block
# by hand, once, and not from this installer.
#
# max_size is a per-site directive and does work: it rejects an oversized body at
# the edge, before it reaches Python.
:8088 {
	@stats path /stats /stats/
	handle @stats {
		request_body {
			max_size 64KiB
		}
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:9099
	}
	handle {
		respond "z2k" 404
	}
}
